{:edb/db-before-t 766 :edb/db-after-t 767 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534079 50 #inst "2026-10-06T02:42:49.689Z" 13194139534079 true] [17592186059487 1100 "Find the inverse of $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139534079 true] [17592186059487 1100 "Find the inverse of $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534079 false] [17592186059487 1145 "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} &= 0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix}- 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+ 2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 0 - (8 - 12) + 2(-3 - 0) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A$.\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= [\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}- 3 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}- 3 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid ] \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & - (-4) & -3 \\\\ - (14) & -8 & - (-4) \\\\ 3 & - (-2) & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/(det(A))C^{T} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{-2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{9}{2} & 7 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534079 false] [17592186059487 1145 "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-(8-12)+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & -(-4) & -3 \\\\ -(14) & -8 & -(-4) \\\\ 3 & -(-2) & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{9}{2} & 7 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534079 true] [17592186059488 1100 "The entry $a'_{31}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ \nequals $1$. Find the value of $k$." 13194139534079 true] [17592186059488 1100 "The entry $a'_{31}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $1$. Find the value of $k$." 13194139534079 false] [17592186059488 1145 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} &= 3\\begin{vmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}- 2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+ (-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3(3k - 0) - 2(-6 - 0) - 2(6 + k) \\\\\n&= 9k + 12 - 12 - 2k \\\\\n&= 7k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{31}$ in $A^{-1}$, we need to know the value of $c_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc_{13} &= (-1)^{1 + 3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 + k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since\n\n$$\nA^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{31}^{′} &= (1)/(det(A))c_{13} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7k} \\cdot (6 + k) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6 + k}{7k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that $a'_{31}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6 + k}{7k} &= 1 \\\\\n6 + k &= 7k \\\\\n6 &= 6k \\\\\nk &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534079 false] [17592186059488 1145 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =3|\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3k-0)-2(-6-0)-2(6+k) \\\\  & =9k+12-12-2k \\\\  & =7k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{31}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7k}\\cdot (6+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{6+k}{7k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that $a'_{31}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6+k}{7k} & =1 \\\\ 6+k & =7k \\\\ 6 & =6k \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534079 true] [17592186059489 1100 "Determine the entry $a'_{23}$ in the inverse of the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139534079 true] [17592186059489 1100 "Determine the entry $a'_{23}$ in the inverse of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534079 false] [17592186059489 1145 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &= -2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+ (-3)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-2(2 - 0) - 3(0 - 0) - 3(0 - 1) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{23}$ in $A^{-1}$, we need to know the value of $c_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc_{32} &= (-1)^{3 + 2}M_{32} \\\\\n&= (-1)^{5}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-1 \\cdot (0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since\n\n$$\nA^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\na'_{23} = \\dfrac{1}{-1}c_{32} = -1\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$" 13194139534079 false] [17592186059489 1145 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(2-0)-3(0-0)-3(0-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{23}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}{M}_{32} \\\\  & =(-1)^{5}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\na'_{23} = \\dfrac{1}{-1}c_{32} = -1\\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$" 13194139534079 true] [17592186059679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277141 13194139534079 true] [17592186059679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277180 13194139534079 true] [17592186059679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277187 13194139534079 true] [17592186059679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277206 13194139534079 true] [17592186059679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277227 13194139534079 true] [17592186059680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277148 13194139534079 true] [17592186059680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277176 13194139534079 true] [17592186059680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277203 13194139534079 true] [175921860596